    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The American Civil War wrought uncertainty about masculinity, femininity, and the future of the patriarchy as many white Southern women began to question the Southern patriarchy and saw an opportunity to redefine their role within it. After the war, women continued to fight for an expanded role in Southern society through women’s Confederate organizations using traditionally female pathways and rhetoric through the United Daughters of the Confederacy (UDC). White Southern women capitalized on Southern ideologies of womanhood and daughterhood in the context of this newly volatile patriarchal structure to strengthen their influence in Southern society. The experience of one member of a West Virginia chapter of the UDC, Mary Calvert Stribling, allows us to recover, with rare precision and specificity, the determined efforts of a group of women to entrench particular narratives of the Civil War into the national consciousness and, specifically, to write themselves into that story. The women of the UDC—Southern, white, and overwhelmingly wealthy and elite—drenched themselves in traditional Southern ideals of femininity and took up the mantle of Civil War memorialization in an effort to carve a space for themselves in the patriarchal reality of the American South. Stribling’s collection illuminates the construction of historical memory of the Civil War and the group of women who dictated, with unparalleled authority, how the Civil War and antebellum Southern society should be remembered.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Sandeen, L. (2024). In Monuments and Minds: The Martinsburg, West Virginia Chapter of the United Daughters of the Confederacy. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/vp935tn3708. https://doi.org/10.25740/vp935tn3708.",
            "type": "preferred citation",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        }
